## 3. Installation on a Linux computer

If you have access to Ubuntu or Debian:

Install QEMU and supporting tools

```bash
sudo apt update

sudo apt install -y \
  qemu-system-misc \
  opensbi \
  xz-utils \
  wget \
  git
```

Check that RISC-V emulation is available:

```cmd
qemu-system-riscv64 --version
```

Check supported machine configurations:

```cmd
qemu-system-riscv64 -machine help
```

Look for `virt`.

## 4. Download FreeBSD

```cmd
mkdir -p ~/lmlm-riscv
cd ~/lmlm-riscv

wget https://download.freebsd.org/releases/riscv/riscv64/ISO-IMAGES/14.5/FreeBSD-14.5-RELEASE-riscv-riscv64-GENERICSD.img.xz
```

Extract:

```cmd
xz -dk FreeBSD-14.5-RELEASE-riscv-riscv64-GENERICSD.img.xz
```

# Inspect the image:

```cmd
file FreeBSD-14.5-RELEASE-riscv-riscv64-GENERICSD.img

fdisk -l FreeBSD-14.5-RELEASE-riscv-riscv64-GENERICSD.img
```

Compatibility note: GENERICSD is a board-oriented disk image, not a guarantee of bootability on QEMU's generic `virt` machine. We should inspect its boot partitions and firmware requirements before attempting to boot it. Don't assume the image can be attached to QEMU and booted directly.

